english-decoding-multisyllable-words-narration-caption-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.590
Show me: I’ll uncover one useful part at a time. In unpredictable, cover un- and -able first so the familiar base predict is easy to read.

english-decoding-multisyllable-words-narration-caption-2
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:15.895
Talk me through it: mark a familiar prefix or suffix, locate the vowel sounds in the remaining base, blend the chunks, then check the sentence meaning.

english-decoding-multisyllable-words-narration-caption-3
00:00:15.895 --> 00:00:21.450
Let’s do one together. For ‘transportation,’ which part is the strongest familiar base to read first?

english-decoding-multisyllable-words-narration-caption-4
00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:29.810
Different example: in reconstruction, start from construct, add re-, then add -ion. The same routine works even though the visible chunks are different.
